# Impact of Geopolitical Risk on Macroeconomic Factors: A Chronological Literature Review

## Introduction  
Geopolitical risk (GPR) – encompassing wars, terrorism, and inter-state tensions – has garnered growing attention for its potential to disrupt economic activity. Policymakers and institutions increasingly flag GPR as a salient threat to the global economic outlook ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=adverse%20economic%20effects,The%20main%20limitation)). Geopolitical events can affect economies through multiple channels: direct impacts (destruction of capital and lives), trade disruptions and supply-chain breakdowns, commodity price shocks, as well as financial-market volatility and uncertainty that dampen investment ([How are geopolitical risks affecting the world economy? - Economics Observatory](https://www.economicsobservatory.com/how-are-geopolitical-risks-affecting-the-world-economy#:~:text=The%20global%20economy%20can%20be,trade%20and%20commodity%20price%20channels)). Taken together, these channels imply that heightened GPR can lead to higher inflation, lower growth, and welfare losses if risks materialize or even merely escalate. While the relevance of GPR to financial markets (via risk premia, capital flows, and asset price swings) and to firm-level decisions (via investment delays or relocation) is acknowledged, our focus is on the broader macroeconomic consequences. We review the evolution of this literature chronologically, highlighting key studies that have advanced understanding of GPR’s economic impact.

Early research on geopolitical and political uncertainty laid the groundwork by examining specific events (such as terrorist attacks or wars) and general uncertainty shocks. More recent contributions have developed quantitative indices of geopolitical risk, enabling systematic empirical analysis. In particular, Caldara and Iacoviello (2022) provide a comprehensive measure of GPR and rigorous evidence of its macroeconomic effects, representing a cornerstone of the modern literature. We begin by detailing their study’s methodology, findings, and contributions, then review several influential papers cited therein. These studies span from mid-2000s analyses of terrorism and war impact to the 2010s development of uncertainty indices, collectively painting a clearer picture of how GPR shocks propagate through economies. The review concludes with a synthesis of insights and identification of remaining gaps and future research directions.

## Caldara and Iacoviello (2022): *Measuring Geopolitical Risk*  
Caldara and Iacoviello (2022) develop a novel **Geopolitical Risk (GPR) index** to quantify adverse geopolitical events and assess their economic consequences ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=perspective%20we%20adopt%20here,involves%20three%20main%20steps%3A%20definition)) ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=it%20as%20the%20practice%20of,these%20events%20materialize%2C%20and%20the)). Their index is built using text analysis of leading international newspapers (e.g. *The New York Times*, *Financial Times*, *Wall Street Journal*) from 1985 onward ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=In%20the%20measurement%20step%2C%20we,and%20The%20Wall%20Street%20Journal%E2%80%94cover)). The authors define GPR as the “risk associated with wars, terrorist acts, and tensions between states that affect the normal and peaceful course of international relations” ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=it%20as%20the%20practice%20of,these%20events%20materialize%2C%20and%20the)). In practice, they identify news articles containing terms related to geopolitical threats (e.g. war threats, terrorist threats) and acts (actual war outbreaks, terrorist attacks) and count their frequency ([](https://www.matteoiacoviello.com/research_files/GPR_INFLATION_PAPER.pdf#:~:text=Caldara%20and%20Iacoviello%20%282022%29,The%20use)). This frequency-based approach, inspired by earlier news-based uncertainty indices ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=In%20the%20measurement%20step%2C%20we,These%20newspapers%E2%80%94which)), yields a monthly GPR index that rises during prominent crises (for example, the index spikes around the Gulf War, the 9/11 attacks, and the 2003 Iraq invasion). An historical extension using archival news sources traces the index back to 1900, showing comparable surges during the World Wars ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=perspective%20we%20adopt%20here,involves%20three%20main%20steps%3A%20definition)). To ensure the index’s accuracy, Caldara and Iacoviello implement an audit process: human readers review thousands of articles to verify that the automated search captures genuine geopolitical risk events and not false positives. They also confirm that media biases or changing news volume do not drive the index’s fluctuations ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=potential%20media%20bias%20in%20the,Using)). A comparison with actual conflict data and alternative risk measures shows the GPR index is a valid and novel proxy for geopolitical threat levels ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=Figure%206%20allays%20some%20of,events%20do%20not%20crowd%20out)).

**Key findings:** Using the GPR index as an observable risk factor, Caldara and Iacoviello examine its macroeconomic impact through a series of empirical models. In U.S. vector autoregression (VAR) analyses, they find that an exogenous rise in geopolitical risk causes **persistent declines in real activity** – notably, lower industrial production, reduced employment, and a drop in international trade ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=vector%20autoregressive%20,market%20response%20varies%20substantially%20across)). These recessionary effects unfold over many months, indicating that GPR shocks can induce prolonged slowdowns. They also find a **short-lived but significant fall in stock prices** immediately after a GPR shock ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=vector%20autoregressive%20,market%20response%20varies%20substantially%20across)). This average market drop conceals important sectoral heterogeneity: defense-related industries tend to **benefit** (experiencing positive excess returns amid higher conflict risk), whereas sectors tied to broader economic conditions (e.g. steel, mining) suffer pronounced declines ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=that%20both%20economic%20policy%20uncertainty,steelworks%20and%20mining%E2%80%94experiencing%20negative%20returns)). The authors further document an international flight-to-safety pattern: **capital flows out of emerging markets toward advanced economies** in response to rising GPR ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=lead%20to%20a%20decline%20in,havens%2C%20including%20the%20United%20States)). In other words, investors reallocate portfolios to safer jurisdictions (like the U.S.) when geopolitical tensions escalate, consistent with increased risk aversion.

A central question in Caldara and Iacoviello’s analysis is whether the economic damage from GPR stems from the anticipation of adverse events (heightened *threats*) or from the actual occurrence of those events (*realizations*). To disentangle this, they construct two sub-indexes: one tracking geopolitical threats (GPR$^{Threat}$) and another tracking realized acts (GPR$^{Act}$) ([](https://www.matteoiacoviello.com/research_files/GPR_INFLATION_PAPER.pdf#:~:text=Caldara%20and%20Iacoviello%20%282022%29,The%20use)). Strikingly, the results show that **threats drive the macroeconomic impact**: *pure threat shocks* (periods of rising risk that do **not** culminate in a war or attack) lead to a protracted increase in uncertainty and a significant, persistent decline in output ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=separate%20shocks%20to%20geopolitical%20acts,for%20instance%2C%20in%20Ilut%20and)) ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=When%20we%20expand%20the%20analysis,pull%20capital%20out%20of%20emerging)). By contrast, when a geopolitical event actually materializes, it tends to **resolve uncertainty**, so the additional economic effect is small and short-lived ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=separate%20shocks%20to%20geopolitical%20acts,for%20instance%2C%20in%20Ilut%20and)). In their words, “the realization of adverse geopolitical events leads to the resolution of uncertainty and produces economically small effects, while shocks to geopolitical threats…induce a persistent decline in real activity” ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=separate%20shocks%20to%20geopolitical%20acts,for%20instance%2C%20in%20Ilut%20and)). This finding aligns with theories where agents react to worst-case scenarios during heightened threats, pulling back on investment and hiring ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=effects%2C%20while%20shocks%20to%20geopolitical,5)). It also resonates with earlier uncertainty literature suggesting that **anticipated risk (the “rumor”) can be more disruptive than the eventual news (“fact”)**, as markets partly price in and adapt to realized events. Overall, their evidence indicates that the fear of conflict is a major mechanism through which GPR depresses the economy.

**Contributions:** Caldara and Iacoviello’s study makes several important contributions. First, it **introduces a consistent, quantitative measure of geopolitical risk** that can be replicated by other researchers ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=suited%20for%20empirical%20analysis,transparent%20methodology)) ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=By%20contrast%2C%20our%20index%20can,7)). This fills a long-standing gap – previously, analysts relied on either coarse, subjective indicators (often proprietary ratings or indices that were not transparent ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=wide,transparent%20methodology))) or event-specific dummy variables. The GPR index, by contrast, is based on a clear definition and methodology, is publicly available, and exhibits meaningful variation over time and across events. Second, the paper provides the **first systematic evidence of GPR’s macroeconomic effects** ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=risk%20,capital%20from%20emerging%20economies%20towards)) ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=vector%20autoregressive%20,market%20response%20varies%20substantially%20across)). While qualitative links between wars and economies have been discussed historically, Caldara and Iacoviello (2022) offer robust empirical estimates of how much output, employment, trade, and capital flows respond to risk shocks. In doing so, they show that geopolitical risk is not just a geopolitical or security concern, but a quantifiable factor that “depresses economic activity” and influences key economic aggregates ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=risk%20,common%20usage%20of%20the%20term)). Third, by separating threat from realization effects, the study sheds light on the **uncertainty channel** of geopolitics. It lends support to theoretical models of precautionary behavior under uncertainty and highlights that managing *expectations* of conflict is crucial for mitigating economic fallout ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=separate%20shocks%20to%20geopolitical%20acts,for%20instance%2C%20in%20Ilut%20and)) ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=effects%2C%20while%20shocks%20to%20geopolitical,5)). Finally, the GPR index has proved useful beyond this paper: it is now used in a variety of contexts (policy analysis, forecasting, asset pricing research) to control for or study the effects of geopolitical tensions (much as the economic policy uncertainty index of Baker et al. (2016) has become a standard tool).

**Limitations:** Notwithstanding its contributions, the Caldara-Iacoviello approach has some limitations. By relying on English-language newspapers from North America and the UK, the GPR index may reflect a **Western-centric perspective** of geopolitical risk. Conflicts or tensions that receive limited Western media coverage could be underrepresented, and the index’s level partly captures media attention intensity. The authors address media-bias concerns and find no evidence that unrelated news crowds out geopolitical news or that partisan slants distort the index ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=potential%20media%20bias%20in%20the,Using)) ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=risk%20aversion%20of%20the%20public,public%20fear%20towards%20geopolitical%20tensions)). Still, the reliance on news-based measurement means the index is a gauge of *perceived* risk, which may diverge from actual risk in some cases. Another limitation is that the baseline GPR series starts in 1985, which, while covering recent decades, misses some historical episodes (the authors’ extended index back to 1900 helps in historical analysis but uses different source material). Additionally, like many VAR-based analyses, the identification of GPR shocks assumes no immediate feedback from the economy to risk in the monthly data ([](https://www.matteoiacoviello.com/research_files/GPR_INFLATION_PAPER.pdf#:~:text=In%20this%20section%2C%20we%20examine,Thus%2C%20any%20contemporaneous)). This is plausible for sudden geopolitical events, but prolonged tensions could themselves be influenced by economic conditions (a potential endogeneity issue). Caldara and Iacoviello mitigate this by noting that most geopolitical crises in their sample are not driven by short-run economic fluctuations ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=Compared%20to%20existing%20proxies%20for,more%20likely%20to%20be%20exogenous)). Finally, the study largely treats global GPR as homogeneous; exploring country-specific GPR indexes (which they compile for 19 countries) and how domestic versus global geopolitical risks interact is an avenue beyond the paper’s scope. These limitations suggest caution in interpretation but do not detract from the paper’s overall significance. 

In sum, Caldara and Iacoviello (2022) establish that geopolitical risk – measurable via news coverage – is a pertinent driver of macroeconomic outcomes. Higher GPR foreshadows slower growth and shifts in financial flows, primarily through raising uncertainty and fear of adverse events. Their work lays a foundation for a burgeoning literature on the economic implications of geopolitical tensions. We now turn to several important precursors and related studies that informed and motivated such analysis.

## Key Literature Cited by Caldara and Iacoviello (2022)  

To place Caldara and Iacoviello’s contribution in context, we review a selection of influential papers cited in their study. These works, presented chronologically, each added a piece to the puzzle of how geopolitical or policy-related risks affect the economy. They range from early analyses of terrorism and war shocks to the development of broader uncertainty indices, and include both macroeconomic and finance perspectives (acknowledging that GPR’s influence spans these domains).

1. **Blomberg, Hess, and Orphanides (2004)** – *“The Macroeconomic Consequences of Terrorism”*. This paper is one of the first empirical analyses quantifying how terrorism impacts macroeconomic performance. Blomberg et al. assemble data on terrorist incidents for 177 countries from 1968 to 2000 and estimate panel regressions to assess effects on GDP growth. The **key contribution** is evidence that terrorism can indeed impair economic growth, but the **magnitude is modest on average**. They find that a typical increase in transnational terror attacks is associated with only a small reduction in annual per capita GDP growth (on the order of 0.05 percentage points) ([The Toll of Terrorism -- Finance & Development, June 2015](https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/fandd/2015/06/bandyopa.htm#:~:text=losses%20were%20a%20tiny%20fraction,percent%20on%20an%20annual%20basis)). The modest aggregate effect suggests that large diversified economies are fairly resilient to isolated terror events, a finding the authors illustrate with the example of the 9/11 attacks causing direct losses under 0.1% of U.S. GDP ([The Toll of Terrorism -- Finance & Development, June 2015](https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/fandd/2015/06/bandyopa.htm#:~:text=The%20dramatic%20attacks%20on%20the,percent%20on%20an%20annual%20basis)). However, their results also highlight heterogeneity: **smaller and developing economies suffer more significant and lasting damage** from terrorism ([The Toll of Terrorism -- Finance & Development, June 2015](https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/fandd/2015/06/bandyopa.htm#:~:text=annual%20basis)). For instance, regions that experienced sustained terror campaigns (such as the Basque Country in Spain) saw materially lower output over decades ([The Toll of Terrorism -- Finance & Development, June 2015](https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/fandd/2015/06/bandyopa.htm#:~:text=But%20the%20effects%20are%20more,growth%20in%20the%20developed%20economies)). **Methodologically**, the study carefully controls for country and time fixed effects, isolating the impact of terror incidents from global and idiosyncratic trends. A notable finding is that developed economies in the sample did not experience statistically significant growth effects from terror events, whereas developing economies did ([The Toll of Terrorism -- Finance & Development, June 2015](https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/fandd/2015/06/bandyopa.htm#:~:text=development,that%20are%20richer%20and%20diversified)), implying that economic structure and adaptability (or policy responses) mediate the impact. **Limitations:** The study focuses on *transnational* terrorism (attacks involving perpetrators or victims from different countries), due to data availability, which may understate the total impact of purely domestic terrorism. Moreover, using annual growth rates may miss short-term market or consumption disruptions that are quickly offset by reconstruction or policy stimulus. The authors acknowledge that while terrorism’s average effect was small, the **psychological and investment climate effects** could accumulate or interact with other shocks – an idea later uncertainty-focused studies would explore. Despite these caveats, Blomberg et al. (2004) established that even non-economic shocks like terrorism belong in the discussion of macroeconomic risk factors.

2. **Bloom (2009)** – *“The Impact of Uncertainty Shocks”*. Nicholas Bloom’s influential paper does not address geopolitical risk per se, but its insights on economic uncertainty are highly relevant and cited by Caldara & Iacoviello as part of the uncertainty literature. Bloom (2009) provides a **structural framework and empirical evidence** showing that abrupt increases in uncertainty can cause short-run recessions. The study was motivated by observing spikes in stock-market volatility after events like the Cuban Missile Crisis and 9/11, which coincided with economic slowdowns ([The Impact of Uncertainty Shocks | NBER](https://www.nber.org/papers/w13385#:~:text=Uncertainty%20appears%20to%20jump%20up,the%20increased%20volatility%20from%20the)). Bloom develops a model where firms face adjustment costs and respond to a rise in uncertainty (modeled as an increase in the variance of shocks) by **pausing hiring and investment**. Simulating an uncertainty shock in this model produces a **sharp drop in output and employment, followed by a rebound and overshoot** once uncertainty subsides ([The Impact of Uncertainty Shocks | NBER](https://www.nber.org/papers/w13385#:~:text=numerically%20solved%20and%20estimated%20using,VAR%20estimations%20on%20actual%20data)). This dynamic – a quick contraction and recovery – arises because firms’ postponement of activity during uncertainty leads to pent-up demand and factor reallocation when clarity returns, temporarily boosting growth. Bloom then tests these predictions using vector autoregressions (VARs) on U.S. data, identifying uncertainty shocks via sudden jumps in the VIX (stock volatility index). He finds that indeed an uncertainty spike triggers a downturn (industrial production falls, unemployment rises) with a rebound a few quarters later, matching the model’s qualitative pattern ([The Impact of Uncertainty Shocks | NBER](https://www.nber.org/papers/w13385#:~:text=numerically%20solved%20and%20estimated%20using,VAR%20estimations%20on%20actual%20data)). **Key contributions:** This work introduced the notion of uncertainty as a distinct type of shock to the business cycle, on par with traditional demand or supply shocks. It provided a **theoretical mechanism (the “wait-and-see” effect)** for why risk and uncertainty – including those stemming from geopolitical events – can depress economic activity even if no fundamental change has occurred. The implication is that **expectations matter**: when businesses fear worst-case scenarios, they cut spending proactively. **Limitations:** One limitation is the reliance on financial-market volatility as a proxy for broad economic uncertainty, which may not capture other dimensions of uncertainty (for example, political or geopolitical risks that do not immediately move stock volatility). Also, the model’s implication of a strong overshooting recovery might depend on the assumption that the shock’s second-moment nature doesn’t inflict permanent damage; in reality, some uncertainty episodes (like prolonged conflicts) could have lasting effects on capacity. Nonetheless, Bloom (2009) was seminal in quantitatively linking uncertainty to macro outcomes. It set the stage for studies like Caldara & Iacoviello (2022) to argue that geopolitical risk induces an uncertainty shock that can slow growth in similar fashion (indeed, Caldara & Iacoviello cite models like Bloom’s in interpreting the protracted declines from threat shocks ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=effects%2C%20while%20shocks%20to%20geopolitical,5))).

3. **Glick and Taylor (2010)** – *“Collateral Damage: Trade Disruption and the Economic Impact of War”*. This paper shifts the focus to large-scale geopolitical events – wars – and their long-run economic fallout. Glick and Taylor examine almost every major war from 1870 to the late 20th century, analyzing their impact on international trade using a gravity model framework. The **key contribution** is a comprehensive empirical estimation of how wars wreck trade and in turn economic welfare. They find that wars have **highly disruptive and persistent effects on bilateral trade** ([Collateral damage: trade disruption and the economic impact of war](https://ideas.repec.org/p/fip/fedfwp/2005-11.html#:~:text=available%20data%20extending%20back%20to,I%20and%20World%20War%20II)). During wars, belligerent nations’ mutual trade flows plummet (unsurprisingly), but importantly, even neutral countries experience trade declines due to broader instability and reallocation of global commerce ([Collateral damage: trade disruption and the economic impact of war](https://ideas.repec.org/p/fip/fedfwp/2005-11.html#:~:text=available%20data%20extending%20back%20to,I%20and%20World%20War%20II)). The authors estimate that even five years *after* a war ends, trade between former combatants remains significantly below its pre-war level. Using their trade estimates, Glick and Taylor infer the impact on real income (welfare) via standard trade openness-output elasticities. A striking result is that the **indirect economic costs of war (through lost trade) are of the same order of magnitude as direct costs like destruction and military spending** ([Collateral damage: trade disruption and the economic impact of war](https://ideas.repec.org/p/fip/fedfwp/2005-11.html#:~:text=and%20neutrals%2C%20controlling%20for%20other,I%20and%20World%20War%20II)). For instance, in case studies of World War I and II, the forgone trade and output for the world economy was enormous, compounding the well-known human and financial costs of the conflicts ([Collateral damage: trade disruption and the economic impact of war](https://ideas.repec.org/p/fip/fedfwp/2005-11.html#:~:text=and%20neutrals%2C%20controlling%20for%20other,I%20and%20World%20War%20II)). **Methodology:** The paper employs panel regressions with country-pair fixed effects and other controls to isolate war’s impact on trade volumes, carefully addressing issues of endogeneity (wars are not random) by controlling for pre-war trends and using global shocks as instruments. **Findings:** Beyond trade, they note that wars tend to depress global economic welfare substantially – the **“collateral damage”** extends far beyond the battlefields. This underscores that peace and stable geopolitical relations are fundamental to economic integration and growth. **Limitations:** One limitation is that the study focuses on trade and does not directly measure other macroeconomic variables (like GDP or consumption) during wars; the welfare impacts are inferred rather than observed. Additionally, the identification of war effects could be confounded by other concurrent shocks (e.g. the 1918 flu pandemic during WWI). The authors also caution that their analysis treats each war equally in terms of occurrence, not weighing by size or intensity – though the results are largely driven by the world wars, which dominate in scale. Despite these caveats, Glick and Taylor (2010) firmly establish that geopolitical conflict is a **critical determinant of macroeconomic outcomes via globalization channels**. Their evidence of long-lasting trade destruction complements Caldara & Iacoviello’s finding that GPR shocks can immediately curtail trade ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=vector%20autoregressive%20,market%20response%20varies%20substantially%20across)), highlighting both short-term and long-term mechanisms.

4. **Baker, Bloom, and Davis (2016)** – *“Measuring Economic Policy Uncertainty”*. This paper, while centered on policy uncertainty rather than geopolitical risk, is cited by Caldara & Iacoviello for pioneering the **news-based index approach** and demonstrating uncertainty’s economic effects. Baker et al. (2016) construct an **Economic Policy Uncertainty (EPU) index** for the United States (and later for other countries) by counting newspaper articles that reference economic policy uncertainty. The **methodology** mirrors what Caldara & Iacoviello later apply: identify relevant keywords in press articles and aggregate their frequency into an index. Baker et al. supplement the news counts with other components (like dispersion in forecasts and the timing of tax code expirations) to validate their measure. The **key contributions** are twofold: (1) providing a novel index that quantifies policy-related uncertainty over time, and (2) linking spikes in policy uncertainty to downturns in economic activity. They show that the EPU index spikes around elections, major geopolitical events like 9/11, financial crises, and contentious policy debates (e.g. the debt-ceiling standoff) ([Microsoft Word - BBD_10March2016.docx](https://www.policyuncertainty.com/media/EPU_BBD_Mar2016.pdf#:~:text=coverage%20frequency,that%20policy%20uncertainty%20is%20associated)) ([Microsoft Word - BBD_10March2016.docx](https://www.policyuncertainty.com/media/EPU_BBD_Mar2016.pdf#:~:text=price%20volatility%20and%20reduced%20investment,and%20has%20drifted%20upwards%20since)). Using firm-level data, the authors find that higher policy uncertainty correlates with **lower investment and employment**, especially in policy-sensitive industries such as defense, healthcare, and infrastructure ([Microsoft Word - BBD_10March2016.docx](https://www.policyuncertainty.com/media/EPU_BBD_Mar2016.pdf#:~:text=battles%20over%20fiscal%20policy,in%20the%20United%20States%20and)). At the **macro level**, VAR analyses indicate that an innovation (shock) in EPU foreshadows a decline in U.S. output, investment, and employment in the following quarters ([Microsoft Word - BBD_10March2016.docx](https://www.policyuncertainty.com/media/EPU_BBD_Mar2016.pdf#:~:text=with%20greater%20stock%20price%20volatility,our%20US%20index%20back%20to)). These results extend to other countries in a panel setting, suggesting a general phenomenon: uncertainty about government policy acts as a headwind to economic performance. **Limitations:** A known limitation of the EPU index is that it may also pick up broader economic distress or media hype. For example, uncertainty often rises *during* recessions (not solely causing them), making causality difficult to establish. Baker et al. acknowledge this and use instruments like newspaper coverage of other topics to argue the index captures more than just bad news. Another limitation is the binary nature of news counts – an article either counts or not – which does not measure the magnitude of uncertainty or distinguish whether the news is anticipatory or reactive. Nonetheless, this paper’s methodology was a breakthrough in **measuring latent economic risks**. Caldara & Iacoviello explicitly build on it to measure GPR ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=In%20the%20measurement%20step%2C%20we,These%20newspapers%E2%80%94which)). By demonstrating that uncertainty indices have predictive power for macro outcomes, Baker, Bloom, and Davis (2016) provided a template for subsequent risk indexes and underscored that **policy and geopolitical uncertainties are important state variables for the economy**. The EPU index has since been widely used, much as the GPR index is now becoming in research and policy analysis.

5. **Hassan, Hollander, Tahoun, and van Lent (2019)** – *“Firm-Level Political Risk: Measurement and Effects”*. This study (circulated as an NBER working paper in 2017 and published in the *Quarterly Journal of Economics* in 2019) develops a **complementary text-based measure of political risk at the firm level**, and is noteworthy for connecting micro-level risk exposure to macro-level trends. Hassan et al. analyze hundreds of thousands of quarterly earnings conference call transcripts of U.S. publicly listed firms, parsing discussions for political risk content. They construct a firm-level political risk (FPR) index defined by the fraction of each call devoted to political or policy risks. Aggregating across firms yields insight into economy-wide political risk perception. **Key findings:** Firms facing higher political risk tend to **cut investment and hiring**, and they increase lobbying expenditures to influence policy ([Firm-Level Political Risk: Measurement and Effects* | The Quarterly Journal of Economics | Oxford Academic](https://academic.oup.com/qje/article-abstract/134/4/2135/5531768#:~:text=stock%20market%20volatility%20in%20a,risk%20increases%20significantly%20at%20times)). This behavior is intuitive – when companies are worried about policy changes, trade disputes, or geopolitical events that could affect their business, they become cautious with capital and labor, and simultaneously seek to shape the political environment. Importantly, these patterns hold even after controlling for general economic news, indicating it’s specifically the uncertainty around politics (not just poor economic conditions) driving the response ([Firm-Level Political Risk: Measurement and Effects* | The Quarterly Journal of Economics | Oxford Academic](https://academic.oup.com/qje/article-abstract/134/4/2135/5531768#:~:text=stock%20market%20volatility%20in%20a,risk%20increases%20significantly%20at%20times)). At the aggregate level, the authors find that the **majority of political risk is idiosyncratic to individual firms** rather than purely common (aggregate) shocks ([Firm-Level Political Risk: Measurement and Effects* | The Quarterly Journal of Economics | Oxford Academic](https://academic.oup.com/qje/article-abstract/134/4/2135/5531768#:~:text=shocks,topics%2C%20in%20the%20following%20quarter)). Even during periods of high overall political risk (e.g. wartime or pre-election uncertainty), different firms experience very different levels of exposure depending on their industries and international footprints. That said, in those high-risk periods the *dispersion* of firm-level risk rises – meaning more firms have unusually high risk – suggesting that **economy-wide risk events do occur and matter** ([Firm-Level Political Risk: Measurement and Effects* | The Quarterly Journal of Economics | Oxford Academic](https://academic.oup.com/qje/article-abstract/134/4/2135/5531768#:~:text=shocks,topics%2C%20in%20the%20following%20quarter)). For example, around the Gulf War or the 2016 U.S. election, many firms simultaneously reported elevated risks. **Contributions:** This paper’s fine-grained measure allows a closer look at the channels: by decomposing risk by topic (trade policy, geopolitics, regulatory, etc.), it shows firms that talk more about a particular risk (say, trade tariffs) subsequently adjust their lobbying specifically on that issue ([Firm-Level Political Risk: Measurement and Effects* | The Quarterly Journal of Economics | Oxford Academic](https://academic.oup.com/qje/article-abstract/134/4/2135/5531768#:~:text=it%20is%20captured%20neither%20by,topics%2C%20in%20the%20following%20quarter)). This links perceived risk to concrete action. Another contribution is highlighting the interaction of micro and macro: economy-wide geopolitical risk (as measured by Caldara & Iacoviello) can be seen as the aggregate of firm-level risks plus a common component. Hassan et al. provide evidence that **common shocks (like geopolitical events) spike aggregate risk which then trickles down unevenly to firms**. **Limitations:** The study is limited to U.S. publicly traded firms, so it misses non-corporate sectors and smaller businesses, as well as any political risks that don’t get discussed in earnings calls. Also, while it covers many types of political risk (e.g. elections, international tensions, regulatory changes), the measure does not disentangle whether the **source** of risk is domestic policy versus global geopolitics – many discussions conflate the two (for instance, a trade war with China is both a geopolitical and policy risk). Nevertheless, the Hassan et al. paper is important in the literature for demonstrating real effects of perceived political risk at the micro level and for offering a methodology that complements news-based indices. Caldara & Iacoviello (2022) cite it to note that their GPR index captures a related phenomenon from a top-down perspective ([Measuring Geopolitical Risk](https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/ifdp/files/ifdp1222.pdf#:~:text=An%20important%20question%20is%20whether,from%20quarterly%20earnings%20conference%20calls)). Together, the two approaches indicate that **geopolitical risk permeates from global events down to firm behavior**, restraining investment and growth.

